@@ -62,3 +62,23 @@ and an object patch applied to a non-object target starts from `{}`. Arrays are
 As the RFC notes, a merge patch cannot set a member to `null`. Neither argument is modified, and the
 result shares no structure with them. It does not throw on valid JSON input. Members named `__proto__`
 are plain data and never reach the prototype chain.
+
+### `createPatch(from, to)`
+
+Returns the JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)) that turns `from` into `to`
+when applied with `applyPatch`, so that only what changed has to be sent.
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+createPatch({ a: 1, b: [1, 2] }, { a: 2, b: [1, 2] }); // [{ op: "replace", path: "/a", value: 2 }]
+createPatch([1, 2, 4], [1, 2, 3, 4]);                  // [{ op: "add", path: "/2", value: 3 }]
+```
+
+Objects are compared member by member, recursively; a value that changes kind (or a scalar that changes)
+is replaced, and the whole document is replaced at the empty pointer `""`. Arrays are compared by their
+longest common subsequence of equal items: an item gained or lost is one `add` or `remove`, and an item
+that moves is a `remove` plus an `add`, without touching the items around it. Differing items at the same
+place are patched in place. The patch is small, not guaranteed to be the shortest possible; it never uses
+`move`, `copy` or `test`. Neither argument is modified and the patch shares no structure with them.
+Members named `__proto__` are plain data.
